From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: miniperf <miniperf@localhost>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 20:53:33 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] i#3544 RISC-V: decode the Zcb compressed instructions

An instruction the decoder does not know makes DynamoRIO deliver SIGILL to
the application at that address, so a userspace built for a profile that
includes Zcb dies under DR at its first c.lbu. Ubuntu's riscv64 glibc hits
one in __tunables_init, which kills every process before main.

Add the Zcb listing and the four compressed memory-operand fields it needs
(c.lbu and c.sb swap the two immediate bits with respect to every other
compressed access, and c.lh/c.lhu/c.sh carry only imm[1]), route quadrant 0
funct3=0b100 and the quadrant 1 100111 block in get_rvc_instr_info(), and
teach the compressed LOAD/STORE fixup that funct3=0b100 is no longer
reserved.

Modelling the accesses as base+disp memory operands rather than as opaque
arithmetic is what lets a client instrument them: a tool counting memory
traffic would otherwise silently miss every Zcb load and store.
